// Core Arena surface — ALLOCATOR STORY STAGE 1 (contract only).
//
// `Arena` is the bounded allocation boundary: allocation is an explicit
// CAPABILITY, never ambient state. `Extent` separately means authority over a
// concrete address-space range; the two relationships must not share a name.
// Arena implementations reclaim their storage in bulk only after ordinary
// multiplicity rules have discharged or moved every stored obligation.
//
// STAGE 1 declares only this contract surface: the trait exists so code and
// designs can NAME the boundary, and so the `alloc`/`dealloc` effects
// (chapter 18 effect names) attach to the machines that will carry them. No
// provider is bound, no runtime exists, and nothing in core requires a
// Arena yet — stage 1 containers (core/fixed_vec.omg) are deliberately
// allocator-free. Stage 2 binds Arena implementations through the reserved
// `Allocation` boundary-provider category, introduces arena-bound
// `Allocation<T>`, and makes `Vec<'a, T>` borrow that storage.
//
// The machines speak raw extents -- `size`/`align` are byte COUNTS (u64),
// addresses are `addr` (the index/count/address model: the two axes never
// conflate; `width(count) <= width(addr)` so the model survives CHERI-class
// ISAs). The typed-container story sits ABOVE this boundary; the boundary
// itself is exactly what a kernel arena or host malloc can honor.

boundary trait Arena {
    machine allocate(size: u64, align: u64) -> addr
    effects
        alloc;

    machine deallocate(address: addr, size: u64, align: u64)
    effects
        dealloc;
}
